Document: ILL - Modular Lineage License (base license)
Version: 1.0 (adapted edition, security-corrected 2026-10-21)
Effective date: 2026-10-15
SPDX-License-Identifier: LicenseRef-ILL-1.0

Corrective revision of 2026-10-21 (corrective, not a new
edition; Section 14.2 continues to apply):
  F1 - 10.1: patent license narrowed to the uses permitted
       by the License Stack (no sell/offer for sale absent
       a Commercial Agreement);
  F2 - 13.1, 13.3: automatic self-reinstatement removed;
       reinstatement requires a written act of the
       affected Author;
  F3 - 1.8: contributions merged without a consent mark
       are not ILL Files and are not licensed until the
       chain entry is confirmed;
  F4 - 1.11: presumption of Commercial Use narrowed;
       express Safe Harbors added;
  F5 - 1.7: API carve-out tightened; wrappers, hosts and
       thin extensions qualify as Derivative Works;
  F6 - 7.1(e), 7.5: source-offer binds commercial
       licensees unless the Agreement provides otherwise;
       network availability (SaaS/hosting) of Derivative
       Works regulated;
  F7 - 6.3(h), 6.7: Modules may not add field-of-use,
       ethical-use or visible-attribution conditions;
       mechanism for exclusion of burdensome Modules;
  F8 - 8.3: test of "independent additions" strengthened,
       burden of proof on the claiming Author;
  F9 - 6.8: compliance may be verified at the level of the
       root LICENSE/NOTICE/MODULES files; recipients
       relying on them are protected from non-evident
       discrepancies;
  F10 - 15.2, 15.10: the Original Author may designate a
       governing law and forum in the NOTICE/AUTHORS file;
       interim and injunctive relief may be sought at any
       time, including during the negotiation period;
  F11 - 1.12: non-commercial hosting (free access, no
       advertising, no promotion of commercial interests)
       expressly confirmed as Non-Commercial Use;
  F12 - SPDX identifier set to LicenseRef-ILL-1.0; Annex
       example (8) aligned with the Safe Harbor of
       1.11(c); 8.1 requires disclosure of non-waivable
       statutory termination rights (14.3) to commercial
       counterparties.

Corrective revision of 2026-10-25 (corrective, not a new
edition; Section 14.2 continues to apply):
  F13 - 1.7, 2: GPL/copyleft adjacency expressly confirmed;
       interaction with strong-copyleft Third-Party
       Components through documented public APIs, process
       boundaries or system calls never subjects such
       components to ILL and never restricts their own
       licenses (no "NC leakage" into adjacent GPL works);
  F14 - 1.8: good-faith recipients relying on the root
       LICENSE/NOTICE/MODULES files are protected where a
       contribution is later found unconfirmed;
  F15 - 9.2: the naming requirement narrowed - prohibition
       of the Product's own name plus the "based on" notice;
       mere distinctness of branding is no longer an
       independent condition;
  F16 - 1.6: authorship of Modifications is determined in
       accordance with applicable copyright law;
  F17 - 6.7: exclusion of a burdensome Module does not affect
       copies already distributed while the Module was in
       force;
  F18 - 15.10: the negotiation period neither suspends the
       13.1 cure period nor precludes notice of termination.

Corrective revision of 2026-10-25, second batch (corrective,
not a new edition; Section 14.2 continues to apply):
  F19 - 4.3, 11.1(c), 13.2, 13.3: internal contradiction
       resolved - removal/replacement of unconfirmed
       contributions (1.8) and entries removed under a
       data-protection order (4.8) are excepted from the
       no-removal rule; immediate termination for removal or
       alteration of chain entries/copyright notices is
       limited to wilful acts or acts not corrected within
       30 days; inadvertent non-prejudicial alterations are
       curable and reinstated automatically upon correction;
  F20 - 10.1: patent license extended to distribution and
       making available for Non-Commercial Use (the 3.1
       copyright grant could otherwise be undermined by
       patents covering the act of distribution); patent
       license binds successors and assigns of the patents
       and survives transfer (anti-escape against shell
       transfers);
  F21 - 5.2, 6.1: the "no additional conditions" rule
       clarified - it applies outside the License Stack;
       Modules under Section 6 are the exclusive vehicle
       for adding terms;
  F22 - 7.1(e): source-code offer must indicate the means of
       access; a written offer may be passed on unchanged by
       downstream recipients (GPL-style offer propagation),
       while it remains valid;
  F23 - 2.3: combination with strong copyleft made operative -
       each affected Author is deemed to grant the copyleft
       license for the combined work, instead of a mere
       declaration that the files "are licensed";
  F24 - 1.8: recipients who obtained the Product before a
       chain entry was marked unconfirmed keep their rights;
  F25 - 10.2: patent retaliation clarified not to apply to
       assertions concerning only Third-Party Components
       that do not claim the functionality of the Product.
  F26 - 7.6: exhaustion / first-sale acknowledgment added
       (17 U.S.C. Section 109; Article 4(2) of Directive
       2001/29/EC): alienation of a particular copy is
       not restricted beyond mandatory law, while the
       license conditions continue to bind as license
       terms to the maximum extent permitted.

================================================================
PREAMBLE
================================================================

ILL is a source-available, modular license built around two
ideas:

(1) LINEAGE. The chain of authorship must remain clearly
    traceable from the Original Author through every
    Modification. Nobody may ever cut, obscure or rewrite
    this chain.

(2) MODULARITY. Every subsequent Author may extend the
    license for that Author's own additions ("Modules"),
    provided the extension does not contradict the base
    License and does not contradict the Modules of the
    previous Authors in the chain. The effective license of
    any file is the License Stack: the base License plus all
    accumulated Modules of the Authors in its chain.

Non-Commercial Use is free. Commercial Use requires a
separate written agreement with the rightsholder designated
in the Authorship Chain.

This License is written generically: it names no company,
website or jurisdiction, and may be adopted by any Author
for that Author's software. It is intended to be enforceable
worldwide, in particular in the United States and in Europe,
under local copyright and patent law.

Everyone is permitted to copy and distribute verbatim copies
of this License.

----------------------------------------------------------------
HOW TO APPLY
----------------------------------------------------------------

1. Keep this full text in the LICENSE file at the root of the
   repository.
2. Identify which files are ILL Files. Recommended per-file
   header:
       SPDX-License-Identifier: ILL-1.0
       Copyright (C) [YEAR] [AUTHOR NAME]
   and/or a per-component list in the NOTICE file. (If
   your compliance tooling requires an SPDX-listed
   identifier, use LicenseRef-ILL-1.0.)
3. Start the Authorship Chain in the NOTICE file (or an
   AUTHORS file) at the root: the Original Author, the date,
   and a brief description of the work.
4. Keep all Modules in a MODULES file at the root, one Module
   per block, each referencing its Author's chain entry.
5. Third-Party Components must be listed in the NOTICE file
   with their own licenses; they are never covered by ILL.

